Experiment 10

Aim:
To write a C program that creates a child process, waits for the termination of the child process, and displays its Process ID (PID).

Software Requirements

• Operating System: Linux / Unix-based system
• Compiler: GCC (GNU Compiler Collection)
• Text Editor: Nano (or any preferred text editor)
• Header Files: <stdio.h>, <stdlib.h>, <unistd.h>, <sys/wait.h>

Theory

• Process: A process is a program in execution. Each process running on a system is assigned a unique identifier known as a Process ID (PID).
• fork() System Call: Creates a new child process by duplicating the calling (parent) process.
	• Returns 0 to the child process.
	• Returns the child's PID to the parent process.
	• Returns -1 if the process creation fails.
• wait() System Call: Blocks the parent process until one of its child processes terminates. Upon termination, it returns the PID of the ended child process.
• exit() Function: Terminated the calling process immediately.
• PID (Process ID): A unique numerical identifier used by the operating system to track a process while it exists.
Procedure

1. Open Ubuntu VirtualBox and start the Terminal.
2. Create a C program using the Nano text editor:bash
nano exp10.c
Use code with caution.
3. Write the code inside the Nano text editor.
4. Save the program using the following shortcuts:
	• Press Ctrl + O
	• Press Enter
	• Press Ctrl + X
5. Compile the program using GCC:bash
gcc exp10.c -o exp10
Use code with caution.
6. Execute the program:bash
./exp10
Use code with caution.
7. Observe the output.

Result

The C program was successfully compiled and executed in Ubuntu. A child process was created using the fork() system call. The parent process waited for the termination of the child using wait() and displayed the PID of the terminated child process.
